# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.3 have "from_weakness"; plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.2 has nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the many call intemperance base and praise temperance and justice out of shame at their own weakness and cowardice" is false. Is S5, "As I have remarked already, they enslave the nobler natures, and being unable to satisfy their pleasures, they praise temperance and justice out of their own cowardice.", true then?
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.2 has "conventions_worthless"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "the conventions of temperance and justice are a mere bauble, agreements contrary to nature, nothing worth" is false. Is S7, "Nay, Socrates, for you profess to be a votary of the truth, and the truth is this:—that luxury and intemperance and licence, if they be provided with means, are virtue and happiness—all the rest is a mere bauble, agreements contrary to nature, foolish talk of men, nothing worth.", true then?
plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2 has "natural_justice → virtue" and "from_weakness → conventions_worthless" and "miserable → ¬happy"; plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.2 has "indulge → ¬temperate"; they differ when only "a man is temperate, chastising his desires and admitting custom, reason and the opinion of others as lords over him", "a man is happy", "a man lets his desires wax to the uttermost and has courage and intelligence to satisfy all his longings (luxury, intemperance and licence)", "this is natural justice and nobility", "the many call intemperance base and praise temperance and justice out of shame at their own weakness and cowardice", "the conventions of temperance and justice are a mere bauble, agreements contrary to nature, nothing worth" and "a man has virtue" are true, which the first allows; plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.2 have "indulge ∧ means → virtue ∧ happy"; plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.3 has "conventions_worthless ∧ (means ∧ indulge → virtue ∧ happy)"; they differ when only "a man is happy", "a man lets his desires wax to the uttermost and has courage and intelligence to satisfy all his longings (luxury, intemperance and licence)", "a man is provided with the means, by birth or a nature capable of sovereignty" and "a man has virtue" are true, which the first allows
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "a man is happy", "a man lets his desires wax to the uttermost and has courage and intelligence to satisfy all his longings (luxury, intemperance and licence)", "a man is provided with the means, by birth or a nature capable of sovereignty" and "a man has virtue" are true and "the conventions of temperance and justice are a mere bauble, agreements contrary to nature, nothing worth" is false. Is the conclusion, "Nay, Socrates, for you profess to be a votary of the truth, and the truth is this:—that luxury and intemperance and licence, if they be provided with means, are virtue and happiness—all the rest is a mere bauble, agreements contrary to nature, foolish talk of men, nothing worth.", true then?
plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2 takes for granted "if this is natural justice and nobility, then a man has virtue"; plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.3 do not
plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if this is natural justice and nobility, then a man has virtue"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: U2: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.2 take for granted "if the many call intemperance base and praise temperance and justice out of shame at their own weakness and cowardice, then the conventions of temperance and justice are a mere bauble, agreements contrary to nature, nothing worth"; plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.3 does not
plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if the many call intemperance base and praise temperance and justice out of shame at their own weakness and cowardice, then the conventions of temperance and justice are a mere bauble, agreements contrary to nature, nothing worth"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: U3: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2 takes for granted "if a man is in a miserable plight, then it is not so that a man is happy"; plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.3 do not
plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2: argue/answered: U3: answered yes, which keeps plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if a man is in a miserable plight, then it is not so that a man is happy"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: U4: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.2 takes for granted "if a man lets his desires wax to the uttermost and has courage and intelligence to satisfy all his longings (luxury, intemperance and licence), then it is not so that a man is temperate, chastising his desires and admitting custom, reason and the opinion of others as lords over him"; plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.3 do not
plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2: argue/answered: U4: answered yes, which keeps plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if a man lets his desires wax to the uttermost and has courage and intelligence to satisfy all his longings (luxury, intemperance and licence), then it is not so that a man is temperate, chastising his desires and admitting custom, reason and the opinion of others as lords over him"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/unfaithful: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2 P4 (S3): the sentence has no word of "a man lets his desires wax to the uttermost and has courage and intelligence to satisfy all his longings (luxury, intemperance and licence)": is it the sentence P4 came from?
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/negation: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2 P7 (S6): the sentence says "no", but P7 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if (a man is provided with the means, by birth or a nature capable of sovereignty and a man is temperate, chastising his desires and admitting custom, reason and the opinion of others as lords over him), then a man is in a miserable plight"
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/negation: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r2 C (S7): the sentence says "nothing", but C denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if (a man lets his desires wax to the uttermost and has courage and intelligence to satisfy all his longings (luxury, intemperance and licence) and a man is provided with the means, by birth or a nature capable of sovereignty), then (a man has virtue and a man is happy)"
